\section{Introduction}
In 2025, Japan experienced the highest number of bear attacks on historical record. A recent research has linked this issue to food deficit because of the disruptions in periodic forest food supply which is usually manifested as masting \citep{xiao2026compound}. Masting is a phenomenon of synchronized production of seeds or other reproductive structures among individuals within the same population \citep{kelly1994evolutionary,kelly2002mast, pearse2016mechanisms}. Masting has important ecological consequences and has long been studied in the context of seed predation\citep{janzen1971seed, kelly1994evolutionary}. By producing large seed crops in mast years, plants can overwhelm seed predators, increasing the proportion of seeds that escape predation and potentially enhancing seedling recruitment \citep{kelly2002mast}. Earlier in the reproductive cycle, synchronized flowering may improve pollination efficiency, which can lead to more seed production later of the season. Throughout the reproductive cycle, reproduction requires substantial resource investment; the resource availability for reproduction may determine the success of a mast event \citep{crone2014resource}. Together, masting is shaped by selective pressures and constraints acting at different stages of the whole reproductive cycle.

Considering different reproductive stages, multiple hypotheses have emerged to explain the mechanism of masting: increased pollination efficiency; satiation of seed predators; constraints associated with resource allocation, and environmental cues or vetos that trigger or interrupt a mast event \citep{pearse2016mechanisms, bogdziewicz2019environmental, bogdziewicz2018correlated}. Our understanding of masting has advanced considerably based on these hypotheses with more data collected and looking at the masting at a finer scale. We have found the necessarily of synchronized flowering which enhance the pollination efficiency. This consists with the finding that wind pollination is more prevalent among strongly masting species because of the pollen limitation for wind-pollinated species \citep{satake2000pollen, pearse2015pollen, bogdziewicz2017masting}. Masting only make sense when there is a density-dependent advantage to overwhelm seed predator and increase seed survival. When the benefits of synchronized reproduction fails in satiating seed predators, the cost of delayed reproduction or missing a good recruitment year will make masting unfavored\citep{silvertown1980evolutionary, qiu2023masting}. More recent studies have also explored different resource-based explanations to investigate how species differ in their capacity to respond to resource fluctuations will affect their masting capacity \citep{journe2023evolution, fernandez2019nutrient}. Environmental cues can synchronize reproductive activity among individuals within a population for a potential good year for recruitment \citep{schauber2002masting, moreira2015effects}, but they cannot, on their own, explain why species differ in the strength of their masting patterns. These mechanisms are not mutually exclusive, and their relative importance may vary among species according to their life history strategies.

It is then important to connect traits and hypotheses with potential evolutionary drivers when considering what shapes the masting behaviour for certain species. Functional traits are closely related to life history strategies \citep{adler2014functional}, which provide a potential strength of connecting theoretical hypotheses of masting to measurable differences among speceis. Traits associated with different hypotheses may help evaluate at which stage the selection is more important in shaping masting that overall benefit the species. By examining these traits in relation to masting behavior, it may be possible to evaluate the relative support for different hypotheses and identify the characteristics associated with variation in reproductive synchrony.

In this study, we investigated the relationships between traits and masting behaviour to evaluate the potential mechanisms underlying masting. Specifically, we tested whether traits associated with different hypotheses explain differences in masting behaviours among species and whether these relationships are similar among closely related species. Here we use North American tree data to test this framework since ecologically and evolutionary they are more closely related.


\section{Method} 
\subsection{Data Collection}
\subsubsection{Trait selection}
We selected traits based on three hypotheses proposed to explain the evolution of masting. For the predator-related hypothesis, we selected traits associated with seed attractiveness to predators and the potential for surviving in seed caches, including seed weight, fruit size (cone size for gymnosperms), oil content, dispersal mode and seed dormancy. For the pollination-related hypothesis, we aimed for traits related to higher pollination efficiency: pollination mode and reproductive type. For resource-related hypothesis, we included traits responsible for higher flexibility in resource use under varying environmental conditions.
\subsubsection{Data scraping}
For our analyses, we obtained the full species list from the two volumes of \textit{Silvics of North America} \citep{burns1990silvics,burns1990silvics2} (hereafter referred as \textit{Silvics}). We extracted masting data primarily from \textit{Silvics}. Pollination mode and seed dispersal data were also obtained mainly from \textit{Silvics}. We obtained seed weight data from the Seed Information Database by the Society for Ecological Restoration \citep{ser2023sid}; gathered seed dormancy data from \textit{Seeds} by Baskin and Baskin and accessed the leaf longevity and oil content data through the TRY database \citep{kattge2020try}. We compiled fruit size and drought tolerance data from multiple sources, including: \textit{Silvics}, the USDA Fire Effects Information System \citep{usfsfeis}, the USA National Phenology Network \citep{usanpn}, and The Gymnosperm Database \citep{gymnosperm}.

We defined masting based on qualitative descriptions of seed production patterns. Although recent studies emphasize quantitative definitions of masting, many forestry manuals view masting as a species-level categorical trait, including \textit{Silvics}. Thus, we classified species according to key phrases describing their seed production patterns. Species described as producing "a good crop almost every year" were classified as weak masting species, whereas species described as producing "a good crop every several years" or having "irregular seed production" were classified as strong masting species.

For some statistical analyses we grouped or otherwise adjusted the data on traits related to masting hypotheses. For numerical traits with a range (seed weight and fruit size), we recorded the full range from the source, and took the mean value for further analyses. We took the log form of seed weight and fruit size because the range of data is large. For categorical traits (pollination mode, reproductive type and dispersal mode), we grouped them into broader categories, For example, we classified pollination mode into wind, animal (birds, bats, or insects), or both. The complete raw and processed dataset are available through KNB.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}
\subsubsection{Masting pattern and single trait}

%emwSep21 -- in both of the next paragraphs the focus feels too much on the f(x) and not enough on the goal/method.... it's almost right but then feels off. I tried to adjust, see what you think and be careful of leading too much with the f(x), as that is of little importance. 
To analyze the relationship between masting pattern and individual traits, we used logistic regression, treating masting as a binary response variable. Given our species-level analyses we used a phylogenetic logistic regression approach (\texttt{phyloglm} function in the \texttt{phylolm} package; v. 2.6.2). To construct the phylogenetic tree displaying the updated phylogenetic structure of our species, we verified all species names using the  \texttt{WorldFlora} package (v. 1.14-5) and updated all species names according to WCVP Backbone data (2025). We then constructed an ultrametric phylogenetic tree in R using the \texttt{phytools} (v. 2.4-4) and \texttt{ape} (v. 5.7-1) packages. To test the phylogenetic correction for each trait, we calculated the half-life using the equation $(\log(2)/\alpha)$ and compared it with the tree height (standardized to 1). This metric represents the time required for the influence of ancestral states to decrease by half. For more interpretation, see \cite{cooper2016cautionary}
\subsubsection{Masting pattern with a multivariate analysis}
In addition to these univariate analyses looking at the relationship between masting and each individual trait, we also conducted a multivariate analysis to test whether traits related to any single hypothesis is a stronger driver of the masting pattern, using non-metric multidimensional scaling (NMDS, implemented using \texttt{metaMDS} function in the \texttt{vegan} v. 2.7-1 package). We evaluated both two- and three-dimendional solutions \(k = 2, 3\) and selected \(k = 3\) based on the lower stress score. To check for potential patterns, we plotted trait distributions on two panels (NMDS1 vs.\ NMDS2 and NMDS2 vs.\ NMDS3).
\subsubsection{Phylogenetic signal for individual traits}
To check for the phylogenetic signal for every single traits as well as masting, we used different metrics depending on the type of data. For traits that were binary, we calculated Fritz and Purvis' D-statistics using the function \texttt{phylo.d} in the \texttt{caper}  (v. 1.0.4) package. For categorical traits, we estimated the Pagel's $\lambda$ using the function \texttt{fitDiscrete} in the \texttt{geiger} (v. 2.0.11) package. For continuous traits, we calculated Pagel's $\lambda$ using the function \texttt{phylosig} in the \texttt{phytools} (v. 2.4-4) package. 
