\section{Results}
\subsubsection{Structure of Data}
In total, our dataset included 188 species, comprising 124 angiosperm species and 64 gymnosperm species. Among angiosperms, \Sexpr{gsub("%", "\\\\%", angio_sum)} of species had masting data available, with strong masting and weak masting species occurring in approximately equal proportions \Sexpr{gsub("%", "\\\\%", angio_mast)}. Among gymnosperms, \Sexpr{gsub("%", "\\\\%", gymno_sum)} of species had masting data available, and nearly all were classified as strong masting species \Sexpr{gsub("%", "\\\\%", gymno_mast)}.

We were not able to find complete trait data for every single trait for all species with masting data. The traits with the most available data was seed weight. The traits with the sparse data were oil content and leaf longevity (Fig. \ref{fig:rawangio} and Fig. \ref{fig:rawcon}).
\subsubsection{Results for Each Hypothesis}
We found relationships between traits related to predator satiation and pollination efficiency and strong masting for angiosperm species. Among the traits related to predator satiation, seed weight was the only traits significantly related to masting (p-value = \Sexpr{round(pWeight, digits=2)}, Table \ref{tab:regressionangio}). Larger seed weight was associated with a greater probability of strong masting (Figure \ref{fig:rawangio}). For angiosperms only, increasing seed weight threefold (from a median seed weight of \Sexpr{as.numeric(medianWeight)}mg to \Sexpr{as.numeric(medianWeight)*3}mg) increased the predicted likelihood of strong masting from \Sexpr{round(p_mastingWeight, digits=2)} to \Sexpr{round(p_mastingWeight3, digits=2)} (Figure \ref{fig:seedweight}). The effect was stronger among species with biotically dispersed seeds, where increasing seed weight threefold (from a median seed weight of \Sexpr{as.numeric(medianBioWeight)}mg to \Sexpr{as.numeric(medianBioWeight)*3}mg) increased the predicted likelihood of strong masting from \Sexpr{round(p_mastingBioWeight, digits=2)} to \Sexpr{round(p_mastingBioWeight3, digits=2)} (Table \ref{tab:regressionseed}; Figure \ref{fig:seedweight}). 

Among traits related to pollination efficiency, pollination mode was the only pollination-related trait significantly associated with strong masting (p-value = \Sexpr{round(pWind, digits=2)}, Table \ref{tab:regressionangio}) for angiosperm. Wind-pollinated species had a predicted likelihood of strong masting of \Sexpr{round(p_wind, digits=2)}, compared with \Sexpr{round(p_animal, digits=2)} for animal-pollinated species. All gymnosperm species in our dataset were wind-pollinated, and gymnosperms contained a much higher proportion of strong masting species overall. This can also indicate that wind pollination is associated with strong masting.
\subsubsection{Multivariate analysis}
Strong and weak masting species were not clearly separated along any axis in the NMDS analysis (Fig. \ref{fig:nmds}). We found no evidence that combinations of traits associated with any of the three hypotheses explained variation in masting pattern.
\subsubsection{Phylogenetic signal}
Masting pattern showed a moderate phylogenetic signal in both gymnosperms (D = \Sexpr{mastGymnoD}) and angiosperms (D = \Sexpr{mastAngioD}) (Table \ref{tab:dstat}).

Seed weight has strong phylogenetic signal for both angiosperm ($\lambda$ = \Sexpr{round(angioWeightLambda, digits=2)}) and gymnosperm ($\lambda$ = \Sexpr{round(gymnoWeightLambda, digits=2)}). This pattern is also evident in Fig. \ref{fig:coneptangio}. Dispersal mode, leaf longevity, pollination mode and reproductive type also showed high phylogenetic signal in Table \ref{tab:lambda}. However, these values should be interpreted cautiously because Pagel's $\lambda$ is estimated differently for categorical and continuous traits, making direct comparisons inappropriate.

\section{Discussion}
We found that masting was associated with traits related to both pollination and seed dispersal, providing support for multiple mechanisms underlying interannual variation in seed production. The positive relationship between wind pollination and strong masting in angiosperm supports the selection for higher pollination efficiency, while the positive relationship between seed weight and strong masting provides evidence consistent with the predator satiation. These associations point to selective pressure acting at both early and late stages of the reproductive cycle, which are critical for successful reproduction and gene transmission.

Previous literature has proposed a positive relationship between seed mass and masting \citep{kelly1994evolutionary}, with one explanation being that larger seeds require more resource investment and simply cannot reproduce every year. In contrast, the other study has reported  opposite relationship between seed mass and masting, suggesting that small seeds are usually associated with seed bank which reduce the fitness costs associated with years of low or failed reproduction \citep{journe2023evolution}. We found a weak but significant positive relationship between seed mass and masting, suggesting that the association between these traits may reflect processes beyond the resource costs of producing large seeds. For predator satiation to favour synchronized reproduction, seed crops must provide a sufficiently large resource to overwhelm seed consumers \citep{janzen1971seed,kelly1994evolutionary}. In some species, seed predators also function as seed dispersers. Larger seeds may provide greater rewards to animal dispersers and therefore facilitate dispersal of these species. At the same time, larger seeds generally contain greater nutritional reserves, which can increase the probability of successful germination and establishment following dispersal, even in the case when seeds experience some degree of predation or damage \citep{bonal2007satiation, moles2004seedling}. Thus, large seeds may provide benefits at multiple stages following seed production, from attracting dispersers to supporting seedling establishment. Seed size and seed crop size are also often subject to a trade-off because greater investment in individual seeds can reduce the number of seeds\citep{castro2024seed, parciak2002seed}. Masting may partially relieve this constraint by concentrating reproduction into episodic years, allowing a species to produce a large number of relatively costly seeds while at the same time reducing the proportion of seeds consumed \citep{jansen2004seed, bonal2007satiation}. Together, these processes could generate selection favouring reproductive strategies that enhance seed dispersal and survival.


\subsection{Scale dependence in trait–masting relationships}
Recent work has emphasized that masting is better represented as a continuous rather than categorical trait, which is mostly available at the population level \citep{bogdziewicz2024evolutionary, bogdziewicz2025dynamics}. The coefficient of variation (CV) is one of the most widely used continuous measures of masting. However, CV might not be a good measurement for masting in some cases, as it does not capture all characteristics of masting and can be strongly distorted by years with zero seed production \citep{bogdziewicz2023measure}. At the same time, evaluating the evolutionary associations between traits and masting requires comparisons across species. If a trait has a sufficiently strong and consistent association with the evolution of masting, we expect this relationship to be detectable at the species level using binary masting data.

Many of the traits considered in our analysis are phenotypically plastic and may vary among populations, potentially in response to local environmental conditions\citep{matesanz2010global}. Thus, traits for which we fail to detect a species-level association with masting may still be important at the population level. There are evidances that some species only exhibit masting under specific environmental conditions\citep{fleurot2023oak, bogdziewicz2025dynamics}. As a result, understanding how reproductive traits are associated with masting may require measurements of both traits and reproductive variation at the population level, considering local environmental conditions and intraspecific variation.

\subsection{Distinguishing selection across types of masting}
Masting has originally been studied primarily as interannual variation in seed production, but we can refine the definition to seed-maturation masting and flower masting \citep{satake2021studying, pearse2016mechanisms}. These two forms of reproductive synchrony occur at different stages of the reproductive cycle: seed masting focuses on the final reproductive outcome, whereas flower masting occurs at the beginning of reproduction. Importantly, variation in flowering and seed production does not necessarily occur at the same time \citep{pearse2015pollen, sork1993evolutionary, isagi1997does}, suggesting that these two forms of masting may be shaped by different selective pressures. For example, we would expect mechanisms related to pollination to perform stronger selection on flowering synchrony, whereas mechanisms related to seed predation, dispersal and establishment may be more directly associated with synchrony in seed production. Separating species according to their dominant form of masting could therefore provide a more refined test of hypotheses underlying reproductive synchrony, and help understand how different selective pressures contribute to the evolution of reproductive synchrony at different stages of the reproductive cycle.

\subsection{Phylogenetic signal limits inference}
We found strong phylogenetic signal for many traits we selected and also masting has a phylogenetic signal. For the analysis we did, it is hard to tease out effect of phylogeny from the correlation between single trait and masting. Also, many traits we included have relatively sparse data, and these data are nested in certain clade. This makes the phylogenetic bias a potentially bigger problem.

\subsection{Deep evolutionary split between angiosperm and gymnosperm}
It is also important to look for the evidence of evolution separately for angiosperm and gymnosperm. Gymnosperms and angiosperms occupy fundamentally different functional spaces. In our dataset, the proportion of species exhibit strong masting is greater in gymnosperm, wind pollination is also universal among gymnosperms. The leaf longevity is also in general longer than angiosperm species. The mechanisms behind masting for angiosperm and gymnosperm might be different. The abundant of certain traits and masting in gymnosperm might indicate this is a more important and efficient reproductive strategy for them, but not necessarily for more diverse angiosperm. There might be interests to test if masting was gradually diluted in angiosperm as they transitioned to biotic pollination and faster life histories.

\subsection{Importance of testing within clade}
Given that the evolutionary mechanisms underlying masting may differ among lineages and operate at different biological scales, investigating evolutionary patterns within clades is an important next step. Previous studies have highlighted the potential of finer scale comparisons to investigate the evolution of masting with in clade or even between species in the same genus\citep{satake2021studying, koenig1996acorn, bogdziewicz2025dynamics}. Clades that contain species exhibiting both strong masting and weak masting such as \textit{Quercus} and \textit{Pinus}, offer particularly valuable opportunities to investigate how traits and selective pressures are associated with variation in masting. To achieve this, we need more comprehensive data on both species-level and population-level for comparison. Such an approach would help our understanding of masting evolution by filling the gaps on how masting varies among populations in response to contemporary ecological conditions and how reproductive strategies have diverged over longer evolutionary timescales. Integrating all these perspectives together will provide a more comprehensive and comparable understanding of how ecological conditions, life-history traits and evolutionary history jointly shape the diversity of reproductive strategies.

